Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||04-03-2001||10:13 PM||||Just noticed when cleaning the engine bay<P>dosnt look too hard but i hear that a bolt or two will break.<P>how hard is the job<BR>and i assume borrow a tap and die set incase<P>Question<P>is this what is causing the low gas milage?<BR><P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||11
Z||000001||c m west||04-03-2001||10:20 PM||||front or rear manifold?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-04-2001||12:09 AM||||When I went to replace mine, I soaked them in WD-40 for a week. (Wasn't driving the car at that time) I didn't break one bolt. If you plan on doing it over a weekend, I suggest spraying them with WD-40 or simular on Friday night then re-spray Saturday morning, and finally Saturday night. Then pull apart on Sunday. On the V-6 the forward one is a huge pain due to the heat shield and the A/C compressor. (You may have to drop the compressor to access bolts) The rear is very easy. I replaced mine several years ago with the Fiero Store Sprints. No more cracks...ever. I would suggest getting a set of reconditioned ones or headers/manifolds. The sprints bolt right in with no other mods to the exhaust. I think Chris West has a set also that does the same thing but less expensive than the Sprints.<P>You can read how I did it at: <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-sprintheaders.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-sprintheaders.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||04-04-2001||09:20 AM||||here are a few forum threads, describing my own experience at replacing manifolds:<P>whats needed to do the job: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008422.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008422.html</A> <BR>a few directions: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007537.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007537.html</A> <BR>and some blood and words: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008206.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008206.html</A> <P>btw, my manifolds have been on now for over a month, and i have no leaks! <BR> ::fingers crossed::<P>good luck!<BR>have fun!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||04-04-2001||11:52 AM||||Can't stress enough soaking the bolts before you begin.  I used Liquid Wrench on my exhaust bolts when I did my clutch and the flanges and pipes came apart easily.  No broken bolts, and they were rusted like crazy!  Replaced them with stainless steel from the local hardware store...  For the manifold bolts, I'd definately soak it well in advance.  If your driving the car, I'd be tempted to soak them each night before you go to bed.  The only problem is your car will smoke like crazy until the Liquid Wrench burns off the manifold, so it's best to do it when you won't be driving the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||kinboyatuwo||04-04-2001||12:20 PM||||Thanks guys for all the help.<P>sorry it is only a four but i will be ready for all the forementioned problems in the topic<BR>looks like a full day event at best<P>kinboyatuwo<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||7
Z||000006||Cheever2||04-04-2001||12:38 PM||||Why didn't ya say so?  I just changed my 4-cyl exhaust manifold 2 weeks ago.  Wasn't too bad, and didn't break any bolts.  Needed 18mm and 15mm sockets.  Take the air intake out of the way, you got 2 18mm bolts on each end and 3 15mm in the middle.  Just loosen at first, cause you'll need to undo the 2 bolts at the flange - this is the hardest part, and since I was also replacing the a/c hose pair which run across the firewall, removing that helped me get to the flange a little easier.  I got a manifold from a salvage, and was advised to have a friend touch up the welding so it wouldn't crack, but the friend showed me that it was already cracked - good thing I took it to him.  Anyway, putting the new one on went a LOT quicker than taking the old one off.  This will take you less than a full day, but plan on it regardless because your legs will get tired from the leaning.<P>I now have a rattling sound since replacing mine, and cannot find out where it's coming from.  Drives me crazy.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||kinboyatuwo||04-04-2001||01:00 PM||||thanks cheever<BR>even more good new for me<P>no air conditioning<P>sound like it will not be too hard after all<P>thanks again<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||1
